25
00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.720
More AI agents could mean more tokens consumed, even as inference gets cheaper.

26
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:52.280
This discussion connects frontier model competition to the harder economic test, whether productivity gains can justify growing AI revenue.

27
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.360
It'll change the whole world.

28
00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:01.640
Benefits will exceed costs, but it will take some time, and the path will be quite a bit more normal than many people would have you believe.

29
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:12.040
Yeah, and just to add to that, I think Tyler's exactly spot on in the fact that like you need to start seeing the productivity numbers in the rest of the economy to justify the revenue increases.

30
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.320
So at some point, the rest of the economy needs to be willing to pay for that product.

31
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:23.160
And that increasing willingness to pay us has to be from increasing productivity in the rest of the economy.

32
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.360
And we haven't really seen too much evidence of that.

33
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.040
Not to say that it won't happen, but it does need to happen at some point.

34
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.240
The other part about is competition.

35
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:44.840
So I think what's starting to happen, but particularly in the last few months, is extreme price competition from the Frontier Labs, as far as like before they were just trying to compete on like who has the better model.

36
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:54.880
And now that the models are being used by increasing number of enterprises, and they're becoming quite expensive because Agentic AI, by the way, uses way more tokens.

37
00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:07.840
So now companies are, you know, open AI anthropic, you know, Muse is in the game, DeepMind is in the game, try to actually say, like, no, we're offering a cheaper model that's as good or maybe even like a little bit better, or maybe a little bit worse.

38
00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.720
And so you're seeing token prices come down a lot.

39
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.520
And so, you know, Ramp has a really nice data set of to track these things.

40
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.920
And you saw exponential increases in token usage and flat revenue numbers, right?

41
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.040
Where's that coming from?

42
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.040
It's because of price competition.

43
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.280
So at the frontier, you're seeing way more price competition than you used to, like six months ago.

44
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.960
And the very recent elections, I think those are being overplayed.

45
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.480
I think a lot of data centers still will proceed, but I think it will slow things down a bit.

46
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:47.440
There's greater likelihood of regulation or somewhat tougher regulation or regulation a bit sooner, or Trump, you know, giving on the issue of guardrails.

47
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:58.880
To me, it's not a a major force, but it again, it's gonna make me a modest bit more bearish on the sector in terms of speed of takeoff, revenue numbers, anything else.

48
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.320
How many investment ideas belong in one portfolio?

49
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:14.160
Investor Paul Enright discusses thematic groupings and diversification versus conviction, a useful framework for thinking about concentrated bets, including on AI.

50
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:17.920
We're not gonna have that portfolio anymore.

51
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.600
Let's replace it with another portfolio.

52
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.800
That's how I've come to think about it.

53
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.000
It's a crude analogy.

54
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.720
There's a lot of merit to that framing of the two different types of businesses.

55
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:36.440
At the portfolio level, what does that imply about the ideas that go into the portfolio that becomes the unit of production for the multi-manager shop?

56
00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.520
Can I torture this analogy a little bit more?

57
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:47.000
Maybe if we switch from like a factory analogy to more of a comedy analogy, and you said a single manager is like a stand-up comedian.

58
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.200
They're going to get up on stage.

59
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:48.840
It's scary.

60
00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.360
They're there by themselves.

61
00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.800
Maybe they have a couple of analysts who are joke writers.

62
00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:00.040
Your job as a joke writer is to give me one great joke that I can stand up and I can stand behind.

63
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.040
That's the equivalent of a single idea.

64
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.560
A multi-manager portfolio manager is more like a showrunner.

65
00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:09.400
I need to produce a bunch of shows and you write the show.

66
00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.360
An analyst has to come together with a packaged show.

67
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.280
It's not one joke in the show.

68
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.560
How does it all fit together?

69
00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.840
You got to tell more stories.

70
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:25.400
I need a package of ideas that go together that will fit into this portfolio because one single idea isn't that important.

71
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:34.760
Part of the reason why that is is because multi-managers are more premised on managing risk under modern portfolio theory, where you want breath, you want diversity.

72
00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:42.280
A single idea has much less relevance in the portfolio of a multi-manager than it does in the portfolio of a single manager.

73
00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.600
Great, you like this idea.

74
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.560
I need six around the same theme.

75
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.480
It's a package, it's a sleeve.

76
00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.080
Different people call it different things.

77
00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.440
You need a lot of those.

78
00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.240
And you put those all together, and that's how you get a portfolio.

79
00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:59.320
Whereas on the single manager side, each individual idea comes together.

80
00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:04.680
The risk happens here because all the ideas might look and be the same.

81
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.080
Then you have hugely high correlation, a lot of volatility, a lot of risk.

82
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:14.480
You've thought about each one of them independent, that you don't realize that they're all the same thing.

83
00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.400
Over here, the risk is that you have five great ideas in there, you treat them like all the other ideas.

84
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.720
It gets diluted down by everything else.

85
00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.960
This is less risky, but maybe you don't capture your best ideas all the time.

86
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:36.080
If we move away from the analogy and go to the day-to-day work, it feels like there's two different functional roles.

87
00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:41.760
There's the analysts coming up with the idea in either side, and there's the portfolio manager putting it together.

88
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.840
How do each of those look different at the multi-manager and the single manager shop?

89
00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:55.040
If you had three units of time and you said someone's got to do the research, someone's got to do the idea generating, and someone's got to do the portfolio management.

90
00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.600
At a single manager, an analyst does almost 100% of the research.

91
00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:08.320
Maybe it's 90% and a portfolio manager does 10%, or maybe the portfolio manager mirrors some percentage of it so that they feel like they're in the sweet spot.

92
00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.520
It's almost independent.

93
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:15.200
You do 100% of the digging, it's your job to cover this, own this, come up with something and say, I think I have something.

94
00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.640
That starts the conversation.

95
00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:22.080
The idea generation process depends on how senior the analyst.

